Falsifies `system_tests::user::receive_cap_attack_tests::a_call_to_a_plain_receive_is_answered_gone_whichever_side_parks_first`.

Architecture: aarch64

The defect is the tree as it stood until §246 (a plain `RECEIVE` never takes a capability),
PROVISIONAL number, calef's ruling A of 2026-10-04 UTC. The patch restores both `CALL` arms as
they were. Receiver-first, `ipc_call_badged` installs the caller's Reply in a receiver parked in
plain `RECEIVE`, which never reads it. Sender-first, `ipc_receive` (through
`collected_without_serving`) delivers the request and leaves the caller parked on a Reply that
exists nowhere, until the rendezvous is torn down.

Expected to fail, on the first (receiver-first) pass: `assert_eq!(got.held_after, got.held_before,
"a plain RECEIVE ended holding 1 slots, having held 0 (receiver_first = true): the caller's Reply
was installed in a table that never asked for it")`. Replayed red on aarch64 on 2026-10-04 (UTC)
against the tree before the fix, with this assertion. Not replayed on riscv64 or x86_64 here; CI's
re-falsify job covers them. The sender-first pass would fail on "the caller was never released", but the
receiver-first pass stops the test first.

**The vacuity guards stay green under the patch.** The receiver parks before the caller runs, and
`words[0] == REQUEST` says the `CALL` really reached the plain `RECEIVE`.

Nothing here is architecture-specific: both arms are portable scheduler code. The badge the
context lines mention is milestone 599 (a frame per filesystem client channel)'s.

